Dawn arrives as a data dependency, and brings the GPU stack with it.

The fourth time this port has watched SwiftShader come back, and the first
time it was invisible to the check that found the other three.

M158 turned off enable_vulkan, enable_swiftshader, enable_swiftshader_vulkan
and use_dawn. M159 found ANGLE's own two switches were not Chromium's and
that angle_shared_libvulkan was a third. M161 found webnn_use_litert bringing
all of it back into Blink. Each time `gn path` named the edge.

This time it did not: `gn path` reported no path at all, because the edge is

  //third_party/dawn/src/dawn/native:sources --[data]--> vulkan-loader

and a data dependency is not a path. Nothing links the Vulkan loader; ninja
builds it, and the validation layers, and 54 objects of SwiftShader's subzero
JIT, so that they can sit beside a binary that never opens them. 83 of the 88
failures the first link of a program against //content had were those three
libraries. --with-data is what makes the edge visible.

Four targets reach Dawn's native library without asking use_dawn, which has
been false in this build since M158:

  //content/utility/on_device_model:on_device_model_sandbox_init
  //services/on_device_model:on_device_model_service
  //services/on_device_model/ml:ml_no_internal
  and the source file behind the first of them

Chromium's own source already carves out one Linux platform that does not
use Dawn here - !(is_linux && enable_cast_receiver) - so this is that same
observation asked as the flag that already exists for it, BUILDFLAG(USE_DAWN)
out of //ui/gl:buildflags.

The other half of that file is a seccomp pre-sandbox hook: it collects GPU
information and fills in sandbox::policy::SandboxLinux::Options before a
policy is installed. has_linux_kernel, for the reason patch 0047 carries.

